      <p class="byline">Technology · Units &amp; fundamentals · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>Internal Resistance vs Conductance: The Same Health Signal in Two Units</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Two battery testers can look like they measure different things — one reads milliohms, the other reads
        siemens. They are reading the same thing from opposite directions.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> <a href="/technology/internal-resistance/">Internal resistance</a> (mΩ) is
        how strongly the battery opposes current; <strong>conductance</strong> (S, siemens) is how easily it passes
        current — they are <strong>mathematical inverses</strong> (conductance = 1/resistance). Low resistance
        equals high conductance, and both signal a healthy battery, which is why conductance testers use the reading
        to <strong>estimate CCA</strong>.
      </div>

      <h2>The inverse relationship</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Battery state</th><th>Internal resistance</th><th>Conductance</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Healthy</td><td>Low (a few mΩ)</td><td>High</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Aging / sulfated</td><td>Rising</td><td>Falling</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Failed</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Aging raises resistance (sulfation, corrosion) and therefore lowers conductance — the same physical change,
        reported in two units. A conductance number falling over time is the same warning as a resistance number
        rising.
      </p>

      <h2>Why testers chose conductance</h2>
      <p>
        The handheld tester works by passing a small AC signal and measuring how well the battery conducts. That
        measurement is <strong>fast, non-destructive, and works even on a partially-charged battery</strong>, and it
        correlates with cranking ability — a battery that conducts well can deliver current. The tester then converts
        the conductance into a <strong>CCA estimate</strong>, which is the number users actually want
        (see <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/resistance-conductance/">conductance testing</a>).
      </p>

      <h2>What the units do not tell you</h2>
      <p>
        Neither resistance nor conductance is capacity. A battery can read healthy on both while holding little
        energy, because the measurement reflects the current path, not the stored amp-hours. The reading is a
        <strong>health proxy</strong> — excellent for triage, not a substitute for a
        <a href="/testing/battery-capacity-testing/">capacity test</a> when the exact energy matters.
      </p>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>I would argue:</strong> The resistance-vs-conductance confusion is a unit problem, not a physics problem —
          and the useful habit is reading both as one signal, while remembering that neither one measures capacity.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why:</strong> The inverse relationship means every conductance test is a resistance test in
          disguise. Users who see that stop being confused by the units — and avoid the real error, which is treating
          a healthy conductance reading as proof of full capacity.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is my analysis, not a verified fact or purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>What is the difference between internal resistance and conductance?</h3>
      <p>They measure the same thing from opposite directions. Internal resistance (mΩ) is how much the battery resists current; conductance (S) is how easily it passes current — conductance is the reciprocal of resistance. A low resistance means high conductance, and both signal a healthy battery.</p>
      <h3>Why do battery testers report conductance instead of resistance?</h3>
      <p>Because a small AC conductance measurement is fast, non-destructive and works on a partially-charged battery, and it correlates well with CCA — a healthy battery conducts well. The tester then converts conductance into a CCA estimate.</p>
